description:
When called from within another package, `dt_eval()` ends up with a root environment that's the other package's namespace (which might not import data.table). It would be nice if dtplyr always worked without intervention by the package author.  But [`cedta()`](https://github.com/Rdatatable/data.table/blob/master/R/cedta.R) always calls `topenv()` so it's not clear how to tell data.table that this specific call is ok (even if other calls from the same package aren't data.table aware).

See discussion in https://github.com/ianmcook/tidyquery/issues/17

@MichaelChirico any ideas?

@hadley I'm just about finished with a `pivot_longer()` translation - I have one last road block before I submit a pull request.

`tidyr:::str_separate()` uses a function called`tidyr:::str_split_fixed()` that calls some internal c++ code for a function called `simplifyPieces()`. I am able to get `pivot_longer()` to work using `stringr::str_split_fixed()`, but I wasn't sure if you would want to add the dependency on stringr for one function.

Do you have a workaround you'd want to use for this? Should I use the stringr function? Or should I add the dependency to cpp11?

I've written a function that takes a data.frame and a character vector indicating which column should be operated on. The function needs to know the class of the column. It works fine when I pass in a data.frame, but if I pass in a tbl_df, the class information is lost. I've got a work around, which is:

```
 if ( "tbl_df" %in% class(x))
{
x <- as.data.frame(x)
}
```

But, this seems less than desirable ultimately.

Here is a reproducible example:

```
 library(dplyr)
x <- data.frame(numericVar = c(1.1, 2.2, 3.3))
class(x$numericVar)
class(x[,"numericVar"])

x <- tbl_df(x)
class(x$numericVar)
class(x[,"numericVar"])

```
